<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shah Abdul Latif Bhittai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1689–1752</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Abdul Latif Bhittai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shah Abdul Latif Bhittai was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> poet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and the most celebrated poet of the Sindhi language. His collected verse, the Shah Jo Risalo, retells local folk romances as allegories of the soul's search for God. He lived under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalhora-dynasty">Kalhora dynasty</a>, and his shrine at Bhit Shah remains a major place of pilgrimage and music.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shah-abdul-latif-bhittai.jpg" alt="Shah Abdul Latif Bhittai" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fitrat69 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shah_Abdul_Latif_Bhitai.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
